            "text": "{\"study_topic_id\":\"study-73e04e2f893db593a981\",\"overview\":{\"text\":\"A nebula (plural: nebulae) is a cloudlike concentration of interstellar gas—mostly hydrogen and helium—and dust. Some nebulae are stellar nurseries: gravity gathers cloud material, and young stars can reshape it while making new stars. Others are matter shed by dying stars or debris from supernova explosions, so nebulae help reveal different stages of stellar life. Their appearance reflects how they interact with light: emission nebulae glow after energetic starlight ionizes gas, reflection nebulae scatter nearby starlight, and dark nebulae obscure light behind them. “Planetary nebula” is a historical misnomer: these expanding shells come from aging stars, not planets. The range from star-forming clouds to stellar remains makes “nebula” a broad family, not a single object type.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/universe-uncovered/hubble-nebulae/\",\"https://cesar.esa.int/upload/201801/ism_booklet.pdf\"]},\"key_facts\":[{\"text\":\"Nebulae are clouds in the interstellar medium, made chiefly of gas—mostly hydrogen and helium—and fine cosmic dust.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/universe-uncovered/hubble-nebulae/\",\"https://cesar.esa.int/upload/201801/ism_booklet.pdf\"]},{\"text\":\"Emission nebulae glow when nearby stars energize and ionize their gas; the Orion Nebula is an example.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/universe-uncovered/hubble-nebulae/\",\"https://cesar.esa.int/upload/201801/ism_booklet.pdf\"]},{\"text\":\"Reflection nebulae scatter nearby starlight, while dark nebulae block light from objects behind them.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/universe-uncovered/hubble-nebulae/\",\"https://cesar.esa.int/upload/201801/ism_booklet.pdf\"]},{\"text\":\"Planetary nebulae are expanding shells of gas expelled by low- to medium-mass stars near the end of their lives; the name comes from their planetlike appearance in early telescopes, not a connection to planets.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/universe-uncovered/hubble-nebulae/\"]},{\"text\":\"A supernova remnant contains material from an exploded star and interstellar material swept up by the explosion; the Crab Nebula is one example.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/universe-uncovered/hubble-nebulae/\"]}],\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Hubble’s Nebulae\",\"url\":\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/universe-uncovered/hubble-nebulae/\",\"publisher\":\"NASA Science\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:49:15.546535+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Nebula composition and role in star birth and death; emission, reflection, planetary, supernova-remnant, and dark/absorption nebulae; planetary nebula naming; Orion and Crab examples.\"},{\"title\":\"The Interstellar Medium: CESAR’s Booklet\",\"url\":\"https://cesar.esa.int/upload/201801/ism_booklet.pdf\",\"publisher\":\"CESAR, European Space Agency\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:49:15.546535+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Interstellar gas and dust composition; emission and reflection nebula mechanisms; dark clouds obscuring visible light; links between interstellar clouds and star formation.\"}],\"research_queries\":[\"site:science.nasa.gov nebula types emission dark planetary supernova remnant\",\"site:esa.int nebulae gas dust emission reflection dark planetary nebula\",\"site:astronomy.swin.edu.au nebula emission reflection dark planetary supernova educational\",\"site:amnh.org nebula interstellar gas dust planetary nebula\"],\"notes\":\"The NASA page directly describes the major nebula categories and their relation to stellar life cycles. The ESA/CESAR educational booklet independently explains interstellar material and how emission, reflection, and dark nebulae appear. The overview is 115 words.\",\"unresolved_concerns\":[],\"self_check_complete\":true}"
